<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of La Marck</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as La Marck family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of La Marck was a noble family from the County of Mark in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, one of whose branches rose to power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> from the 15th century. Its members gained control of the Duchy of Bouillon and the principality of Sedan, which they ruled as near-independent lords. Through the marriage of the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-de-la-marck">Charlotte de La Marck</a>, these lands passed to Henri de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-tour-dauvergne">La Tour d'Auvergne</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck.jpg" alt="House of La Marck" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_House_of_La_Marck.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family descended from Count Adolf I, of a cadet branch of the Rhenish Berg dynasty seated at Altena Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. Adolf inherited the fortress of Mark, in present-day Hamm-Uentrop, from his father Count Frederick I of Berg-Altena, who died in 1198, together with the older county around Altena. He moved there in the early 13th century, began to call himself count de La Mark and is first documented as comes de Marca in 1202. The family had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ministerialis">ministeriales</a> of the Archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the height of its power the family ruled the County of Mark and the duchies of Jülich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, Berg and Guelders, as well as the County of Ravensberg. Adolf III inherited Cleves from his maternal uncle in 1368 and Mark from his brother in 1391, and his line later ruled the United Duchies of Jülich-Cleves-Berg until John William died in 1609, which was followed by the War of the Jülich succession. Members of the house were prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Liège, Münster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a> and archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Anne of Cleves, fourth wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a>, was a daughter of John III.</p><p>A branch descended from Eberhard I, Lord of Arenberg, held Sedan. William I de La Marck assassinated a Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Liège, setting off a civil war there; his nephew Érard became prince-bishop in 1506. Robert III de la Marck was made Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1526, and William II de la Marck led the sea beggars in the Eighty Years' War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Charlotte de la Marck married Henri de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-tour-dauvergne">La Tour d'Auvergne</a>, Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in 1591 and died without issue in 1594, and her claims to Bouillon passed to her husband. The house died out in the male line in 1773 with Louis Engelbert, Count de la Marck. His daughter Louise-Marguerite married Charles Marie Raymond, Duke of Arenberg, and the title passed to their descendants in the House of Arenberg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of La Marck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the mid-15th century the <b>La Marck family</b> gained control of it, and by the 1480s they ruled it in practice as an independent duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bouillon">Duchy of Bouillon</a></li><li><blockquote>Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne, a Huguenot leader, married into the house of <b>La Marck</b> and became prince of Sedan.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-turenne">Viscounty of Turenne</a></li></ul></section>
